  "notes": "A pop-rock lead voice moved wholesale into Nashville arrangements. The tempo band is the entry's own headline fact — \"BPM 71–118 straight on 2&4, tightly clustered — this is the *narrowest* tempo distribution in the section (most singles 72–93)\" — so the band centres at 84 with a ceiling that barely reaches the genre's mode. The drums encode a stated negative: \"very low fill density — the groove is deliberately static,\" which is the longest fill spacing and the lowest hat fill in this batch, over \"cross-stick verses, snare from the chorus, tambourine 8ths; kick on 1 and 3.\" Harmony is quoted almost as a joke about itself: \"I–V–vi–IV almost to the exclusion of everything else, with I–IV–V on the roots cuts,\" so the top family carries a weight no other model here gives a single progression. The bass is \"root-fifth, long notes; mixed round and warm rather than tight.\" The counter is the gospel-adjacent background stack the entry calls \"the signature layer,\" plus fiddle and mandolin \"taking the answer lines.\" Melody is \"baritone with a distinctive rasp, mid-register, narrow range, long note values.\" ⚠ Confidence is medium, matching the research; the per-track readings are [LOW-CONF]. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are the velocity tiers, the perc density and the counter ratio.",
